\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {No cheap signal grades beyond the free correctness bit: every reference-matching metric lands in $[-0.33, +0.14]$ against blind human soundness, the verification signals carry real signal, and the free machine-checked answer-correct bit tops the entire field at $+0.92$ $[+0.78, +0.99]$.} Each row is one evaluation signal, scored against blind human soundness grades on the same anchor set of 60 human-graded solutions: its Pearson correlation (bootstrap 95\% CI) and Spearman, its within-correct correlation (restricted to solutions whose final answer is correct, the margin a grader must win), its model-ranking boundary (pooled cross-family Spearman, hard versus saturated benchmarks), and its variance decomposition (item / family-style / residual shares). Prefix repair truncates a solution, lets a fixed completer finish it, and scores whether the completion reaches the right answer. Nothing beats the free bit: the prefix-repair curve mean's partial correlation with soundness given the bit is $-0.10$ $[-0.30,+0.22]$, while the bit's partial given the curve retains $+0.77$ $[+0.55,+0.95]$ (Section~\ref {sec:verify}). Notes: gold-token logprob uses the $n{=}42$ subset with logprob available; within-correct entries use $n{=}48$ ($n{=}32$ for logprob); the free bit is constant within the correct-only subset, so its within-correct entry is undefined by construction; unigram-F1's hard-benchmark inversion ($-0.49$) is a verbosity confound, moving to $-0.07$ when output length is controlled; the five-metric battery carries no multiplicity correction, and the judge rows' within-correct caveats are in Sections~\ref {sec:verify} and~\ref {sec:limits}.}}{2}{table.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:classfull}{{1}{2}{\textbf {No cheap signal grades beyond the free correctness bit: every reference-matching metric lands in $[-0.33, +0.14]$ against blind human soundness, the verification signals carry real signal, and the free machine-checked answer-correct bit tops the entire field at $+0.92$ $[+0.78, +0.99]$.} Each row is one evaluation signal, scored against blind human soundness grades on the same anchor set of 60 human-graded solutions: its Pearson correlation (bootstrap 95\% CI) and Spearman, its within-correct correlation (restricted to solutions whose final answer is correct, the margin a grader must win), its model-ranking boundary (pooled cross-family Spearman, hard versus saturated benchmarks), and its variance decomposition (item / family-style / residual shares). Prefix repair truncates a solution, lets a fixed completer finish it, and scores whether the completion reaches the right answer. Nothing beats the free bit: the prefix-repair curve mean's partial correlation with soundness given the bit is $-0.10$ $[-0.30,+0.22]$, while the bit's partial given the curve retains $+0.77$ $[+0.55,+0.95]$ (Section~\ref {sec:verify}). Notes: gold-token logprob uses the $n{=}42$ subset with logprob available; within-correct entries use $n{=}48$ ($n{=}32$ for logprob); the free bit is constant within the correct-only subset, so its within-correct entry is undefined by construction; unigram-F1's hard-benchmark inversion ($-0.49$) is a verbosity confound, moving to $-0.07$ when output length is controlled; the five-metric battery carries no multiplicity correction, and the judge rows' within-correct caveats are in Sections~\ref {sec:verify} and~\ref {sec:limits}}{table.caption.1}{}}
